// Verification half of the record_integrity lane: read every stored
// record back, recompute each attestation fingerprint over the canonical
// serialization, and walk the per-case prev_event chains; a Detection
// Finding (2004) drops onto stdout for every break (fingerprint mismatch
// = content tampered post-stamp, chain-head-missing / link-mismatch =
// deletion or splice). The stored records come back through the
// catch-all reconstruction: ClickHouse has re-sorted the JSON keys, and
// the operator's sort()-canonicalized serialization is what makes the
// recompute match what attest stamped.
//
// TODO (scheduling + alerting, tracked in
// docs/issues/tenzir-record-integrity-attestations.md): as wired, this
// pipeline is standing — the node runs it once at startup (a bounded
// from_clickhouse read-back completes and exits), and restart-on-error
// only retries failures. The issue's verification lane wants a scheduled
// walk (a cron "<schedule>" { ... } wrapper, intel-node shape) feeding
// the existing Grafana contact-point rails; both decisions are the
// operator's. Until then, break findings are visible in the pod logs and
// the node's pipeline diagnostics.
from_clickhouse table="investigations.records",
  host="clickhouse-tenzir.clickhouse.svc.cluster.local",
  port=9000,
  user="investigations-reader",
  password=secret("investigations-reader-password"),
  tls=false
homelab::investigations::ocsf::verify
to_stdout { write_json }
